#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# solve

#% text
# `solve(eq, x)` gives the solutions of the equation `eq` for `x` as a list of rule lists `[[x -> a], [x -> b], ...]`.

#% text
# `solve([eq1, eq2, ...], [x, y, ...])` solves a system of equations for several unknowns.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# An equation is written with `==`. An expression without `==` is taken as equal to zero.

#% item
# Each solution is a list of rules, one per unknown, ready to be used with `/.` or `rewrite`.

#% item
# Polynomial equations of degree at most 4 are solved by radicals; higher degrees give `root` objects, exact descriptions of the roots that `n` evaluates to numbers.

#% item
# Roots are listed with their multiplicity: a double root appears twice.

#% item
# Rational, radical and simple transcendental equations are solved too; for periodic functions the principal solution is given.

#% item
# Systems of polynomial equations are solved by elimination (Groebner bases). Equations with parameters give the generic solutions, valid when no denominator vanishes.

#% item
# An equation with no solution gives the empty list.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The two roots of a quadratic:

#% cell
solve(x^2 - 5x + 6 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 2], [x -> 3]]

#% text
# Complex roots:

#% cell
solve(x^2 + 1 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> -1im], [x -> 1im]]

#% text
# A linear system:

#% cell
solve([x + y == 3, x - y == 1], [x, y])
#% output
#= [[x -> 2, y -> 1]]

#% text
# A transcendental equation:

#% cell
solve(exp(x) == 2, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> log(2)]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The general quadratic, in terms of its coefficients:

#% cell
solve(x^2 + p*x + q == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> (1/2)*(-p - (-4*q + p^2)^(1/2))], [x -> (1/2)*(-p + (-4*q + p^2)^(1/2))]]

#% text
# A cubic with integer roots, and one with radicals:

#% cell
solve(x^3 - 6x^2 + 11x - 6 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 1], [x -> 2], [x -> 3]]

#% cell
solve(x^3 - 2 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> -1*(-1)^(1/3)*2^(1/3)], [x -> 1im*(-1)^(1/6)*2^(1/3)], [x -> 2^(1/3)]]

#% text
# A quintic, whose roots are described exactly by `root` objects:

#% cell
solve(x^5 - x + 1 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> root(x => 1 - x + x^5, 1)], [x -> root(x => 1 - x + x^5, 2)], [x -> root(x => 1 - x + x^5, 3)], [x -> root(x => 1 - x + x^5, 4)], [x -> root(x => 1 - x + x^5, 5)]]

#% text
# A radical equation:

#% cell
solve(sqrt(x + 1) == 3, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 8]]

#% text
# A nonlinear system:

#% cell
solve([x^2 + y^2 == 1, x == y], [x, y])
#% output
#= [[x -> (-1/2)*2^(1/2), y -> (-1/2)*2^(1/2)], [x -> (1/2)*2^(1/2), y -> (1/2)*2^(1/2)]]

#% text
# An expression without `==` is set equal to zero:

#% cell
solve(x^4 - 1, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> -1], [x -> -1im], [x -> 1im], [x -> 1]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The values of the solutions, by putting each solution into `x`:

#% cell
solve(x^2 == 4, x) |> map(s => x /. s, _)
#% output
#= [-2, 2]

#% text
# The golden ratio is the positive root of x^2 = x + 1:

#% cell
solve(x^2 - x - 1 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> (1/2)*(1 - 5^(1/2))], [x -> (1/2)*(1 + 5^(1/2))]]

#% text
# Numerical values of the roots of a quintic:

#% cell
n(solve(x^5 - x + 1 == 0, x))
#% output
#= [[x -> -1.1673039782614187], [x -> complex(-0.18123244446987535, -1.0839541013177107)], [x -> complex(-0.18123244446987535, 1.0839541013177107)], [x -> complex(0.7648844336005847, -0.3524715460317262)], [x -> complex(0.7648844336005847, 0.3524715460317262)]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A double root is listed twice:

#% cell
solve(x^2 - 2x + 1 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 1], [x -> 1]]

#% text
# An equation with no solution:

#% cell
solve(x == x + 1, x)
#% output
#= []

#% text
# An equation that is not polynomial, with conditions bounding the unknown: the real roots in that interval

#% cell
solve(sin(x) == 0 and 0 < x and x < 7, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> pi], [x -> 2*pi]]

#% text
# Roots without a closed form are numbers:

#% cell
solve(bessel_j(0, x) == 0 and 0 < x and x < 6, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 2.4048255576957724], [x -> 5.520078110286311]]

#% text
# Facts about the unknowns with the `assumptions:` option:

#% cell
solve(x^2 == 4, x, assumptions: x > 0)
#% output
#= [[x -> 2]]

#% text
# The solutions go into other functions through `rewrite`:

#% cell
sol = solve(x^2 - 3*x + 1 == 0, x); simplify(rewrite(x^2 - 3*x + 1, sol))
#% output
#= [0, 0]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Solutions with parameters are generic: this one assumes a is not zero.

#% cell
solve(a*x + b == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> -1*b/a]]

#% text
# Only the principal solution of a periodic equation is given:

#% cell
solve(sin(x) == 1/2, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> (1/6)*pi]]

#% text
# A list of solutions is not itself a rule list; map over it to use each solution:

#% cell
x /. solve(x^2 == 4, x)
#% output
#= [-2, 2]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `find_root`, `factor`, `n`, `rewrite`
